// The RBA namespace schema for Principal-Graph's own grant data — what
// src/exporters/rba.ts's own header describes in prose, made real and
// publishable. Not published automatically by anything in this repo (a
// deliberate choice — see src/exporters/rba.ts's header and README.md's
// "Sync grants into RBA" section): publish it yourself, once, against
// your own RBA deployment, before running `npm run export:rba` for the
// first time:
//
//   authz schema publish rba/principal-graph.authz
//
// One namespace per `resource.kind` this repo's adapters actually produce
// today — never a speculative one for a kind nothing here creates yet
// (same discipline as src/capabilities.ts's TOOL_CAPABILITIES: a
// namespace nothing writes to is a guess, not a fact). Adding a new
// adapter that introduces a new resource kind, or a new relation on an
// existing one, means extending this file too — the exporter maps
// whatever `grant_edge.relation` value it finds straight through, so an
// unpublished relation on an otherwise-known namespace fails the write
// with `tuple_validation_failed` rather than silently doing nothing.
//
// Every namespace also declares `any_access`, a union of everything that
// namespace grants — the same "what can this thing reach, at all" question
// this whole project exists to answer, answerable with one check regardless
// of which relation vocabulary a given resource kind happens to use:
//   authz check principal:<source>:<id> any_access tool:<source>:<id>
//   authz check principal:<source>:<id> any_access repo:<source>:<id>
//   authz check principal:<source>:<id> any_access bucket:<source>:<id>

// Produced by src/adapters/mcp-config.ts and src/adapters/broker-audit-sink.ts.
namespace tool {
  relation can_call: principal

  permission any_access = can_call
}

// Produced by src/adapters/github-collaborators.ts. GitHub's five
// permission levels (pull/triage/push/maintain/admin) are already
// collapsed onto these three relations before they ever reach RBA — see
// relationFromPermissions() in src/adapters/github-collaborators.ts.
namespace repo {
  relation read: principal
  relation write: principal
  relation admin: principal

  permission any_access = read | write | admin
}

// Produced by src/adapters/aws-s3.ts. AWS's own IAM Policy Simulator
// result is already collapsed onto these three relations before it ever
// reaches RBA — see RELATION_CHECKS in src/adapters/aws-s3.ts.
namespace bucket {
  relation read: principal
  relation write: principal
  relation admin: principal

  permission any_access = read | write | admin
}

// Produced by src/adapters/workspace-groups.ts. Google's own three
// membership roles, lowercased — see relationFromRole() in
// src/adapters/workspace-groups.ts.
//
// Every relation's subject is `principal`, deliberately never a subject
// SET (`principal | group#member`, letting one group nest inside
// another) — a closed design question, not an open one. Google's own
// `includeDerivedMembership` already resolves nested-group membership
// into a flat list of real users before this adapter ever sees it, and a
// nested `type: 'GROUP'` entry is skipped outright (see
// workspace-groups.ts's own header) — so this project never actually
// HAS group-nesting data to express as a subject-set rewrite in the
// first place. Modeling `group#member` here anyway, against data that
// doesn't exist, would duplicate territory RBA already owns for no
// adapter's real output (the same reasoning src/policies.ts gives for
// not building a second policy DSL), and nothing downstream would
// consume it either: this exporter is one-way (`grant_edge` → RBA), and
// no report/policy in this repo ever queries RBA back for a transitive
// result. Revisit only if a real adapter starts producing actual
// group-nesting data that some source API doesn't already flatten.
namespace group {
  relation owner: principal
  relation manager: principal
  relation member: principal

  permission any_access = owner | manager | member
}

// Produced by src/adapters/postgres-roles.ts. A target database's own
// tier-role membership (read/write/admin), checked via pg_has_role() —
// see that file's own header on why there's no auto-detected human/
// service distinction here the way an AWS ARN or a GitHub Bot type has.
namespace db {
  relation read: principal
  relation write: principal
  relation admin: principal

  permission any_access = read | write | admin
}
